# SYNOPSIS

```usage
fold [-bs] [-w num | -num] [file ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`fold` copies each <file>, or standard input if there is none, to standard output, breaking every line longer than 80 columns by putting a newline where it reaches that width; a <file> of `-` is standard input.

Columns are counted in characters, a UTF-8 sequence as one.
A tab moves to the next multiple of 8 columns, a backspace moves back one and a carriage return goes back to the first; other control characters take no room.

: `-b`
  Count bytes, not columns: tabs, backspaces and carriage returns count one each, as other bytes do, and a UTF-8 sequence is never split.
: `-s`
  Break a long line after the last blank before the width, when there is one, rather than at the width.
: `-w` <num>, `-`<num>
  Break at <num> columns instead of 80.

`fold` is sbase's, and found only in the POSIX namespace: `/boot/bin/posix/fold` is a hard link to `sbase-box`, which runs the command its name names, and `/lib/ns/posix` (a manifest's `ns=posix`, see namespace(6)) binds `/boot/bin/posix` before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program that runs `fold` finds it there.
There is no native `fold`.

# SEE ALSO

expand(1), cut(1); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/fold.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

A <file> that cannot be opened is reported on standard error, as `fold: fopen /nothing: No such file or directory`, and the next is read; `fold` then exits 1.
Input that is not valid UTF-8 ends `fold` with `fold: charntorune: file: invalid utf`, exit status 1, as does a read error.
A width of 0 is refused, as `strtonum 0: too small` or `illegal width value, too small`; a bad flag prints the usage message; both exit 1.
Otherwise `fold` exits 0.

# BUGS

Without `-b`, every character is one column wide, so wide characters, such as most CJK ones, and combining ones are measured wrongly.

Input that is not UTF-8 cannot be folded at all, even with `-b`.

Untested on VectraOS.
